### Why is two-phase commit the wrong answer?

Two-phase commit needs every participant to be an XA-capable transactional resource. External banking partners expose REST or messaging APIs, not XA resources, so you cannot enroll them. Even if you could, 2PC is blocking: prepared participants hold locks until the coordinator decides, and if the coordinator crashes, they are stuck in doubt.

```java
package com.bank.payment.antipattern;

// WRONG: attempting 2PC across external REST partners
public class TwoPhaseCommitWrong {
    // Partner A: REST API at https://partner-a.com/api/transfer
    // Partner B: REST API at https://partner-b.com/api/transfer
    // These are NOT XA resources. You cannot:
    //   1. "prepare" them (they have no prepare endpoint)
    //   2. "commit" or "rollback" them atomically
    //   3. Hold locks across independent organizations
    // If the coordinator dies after "prepare", both partners are stuck.
}
```

### How does the per-partner bulkhead work?

Each partner gets its own queue and dedicated consumer pool, so a down partner's backlog does not starve the healthy ones.

```java
package com.bank.payment.resilience;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class PartnerBulkhead {

    private final Map<String, ThreadPoolExecutor> partnerPools;

    public PartnerBulkhead() {
        partnerPools = Map.of(
            "HDFC", createPool("HDFC"),
            "ICICI", createPool("ICICI"),
            "SBI", createPool("SBI")
        );
    }

    private ThreadPoolExecutor createPool(String partner) {
        return new ThreadPoolExecutor(
            5, 10, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
            new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(50) // bounded queue per partner
        );
    }

    public void submitToPartner(String partner, Runnable task) {
        ThreadPoolExecutor pool = partnerPools.get(partner);
        if (pool == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown partner: " + partner);
        pool.submit(task); // If HDFC is down, only HDFC's queue fills up
    }
}
```

### How does the circuit breaker detect failure early?

Each partner has a breaker that counts recent failures. When they cross a threshold, the breaker opens and subsequent calls fail immediately instead of waiting on a doomed call.

```java
package com.bank.payment.resilience;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class SimpleCircuitBreaker {

    private enum State { CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN }

    private State state = State.CLOSED;
    private final AtomicInteger failureCount = new AtomicInteger(0);
    private final int threshold = 5;
    private final long cooldownMs = 30_000;
    private Instant openedAt;

    public boolean allowRequest() {
        if (state == State.CLOSED) return true;
        if (state == State.OPEN) {
            if (Instant.now().toEpochMilli() - openedAt.toEpochMilli() > cooldownMs) {
                state = State.HALF_OPEN;
                return true; // trial request
            }
            return false; // fail fast
        }
        return true; // HALF_OPEN: allow one trial
    }

    public void recordSuccess() {
        failureCount.set(0);
        state = State.CLOSED;
    }

    public void recordFailure() {
        if (failureCount.incrementAndGet() >= threshold) {
            state = State.OPEN;
            openedAt = Instant.now();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does transaction-ID idempotency prevent double-debit?

Every leg carries the transaction ID. Each partner interaction records the result. On retry, the stored result is returned instead of debiting again.

```java
package com.bank.payment.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class IdempotentPartnerClient {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> processedTransactions =
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public String transferToPartner(String transactionId, String partnerId,
                                     BigDecimal amount) {
        // Check if already processed
        String existingResult = processedTransactions.get(transactionId);
        if (existingResult != null) {
            System.out.println("Idempotent hit: " + transactionId + " -> " + existingResult);
            return existingResult; // No double-debit
        }

        // Execute the transfer
        String result = callPartnerApi(partnerId, transactionId, amount);
        processedTransactions.put(transactionId, result);
        return result;
    }

    private String callPartnerApi(String partnerId, String txnId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // REST call to partner
        return "SUCCESS";
    }
}
```

```text
# First call: transferToPartner("TXN-001", "HDFC", 50000) -> executes, returns SUCCESS
# Retry call: transferToPartner("TXN-001", "HDFC", 50000) -> idempotent hit, returns SUCCESS
# Rajesh is never double-debited.
```

### How does the Saga with compensations work?

The payment is modeled as a sequence of local steps with compensating reversals. Money is held in a durable in-flight escrow tied to the transaction ID.

```java
package com.bank.payment.saga;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CrossBorderPaymentSaga {

    private final IdempotentPartnerClient partnerClient;
    private final EscrowService escrowService;

    public CrossBorderPaymentSaga(IdempotentPartnerClient partnerClient,
                                   EscrowService escrowService) {
        this.partnerClient = partnerClient;
        this.escrowService = escrowService;
    }

    public void execute(String txnId, String senderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Step 1: Debit sender into escrow (money is always accounted for)
        escrowService.debitToEscrow(txnId, senderId, amount);

        // Step 2: Transfer leg 1 (HDFC)
        String leg1 = partnerClient.transferToPartner(txnId + "-L1", "HDFC", amount);
        if (!"SUCCESS".equals(leg1)) {
            // Compensation: refund sender from escrow
            escrowService.refundFromEscrow(txnId, senderId, amount);
            return;
        }

        // Step 3: Transfer leg 2 (ICICI)
        String leg2 = partnerClient.transferToPartner(txnId + "-L2", "ICICI", amount);
        if (!"SUCCESS".equals(leg2)) {
            // Compensation: reverse leg 1 + refund sender
            partnerClient.transferToPartner(txnId + "-L1-REV", "HDFC", amount.negate());
            escrowService.refundFromEscrow(txnId, senderId, amount);
            return;
        }

        // All legs complete: release escrow
        escrowService.releaseEscrow(txnId);
    }
}
```

### How does reconciliation close the loop?

A periodic process compares your ledger against each partner's ledger by transaction ID and resolves discrepancies.

```java
package com.bank.payment.reconciliation;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class ReconciliationJob {

    public void reconcile(String partnerId) {
        List<String> ourPending = getOurPendingTransactions(partnerId);
        for (String txnId : ourPending) {
            String partnerStatus = queryPartnerStatus(partnerId, txnId);
            if ("COMPLETED".equals(partnerStatus)) {
                markComplete(txnId);
            } else if ("NOT_FOUND".equals(partnerStatus)) {
                retryOrCompensate(txnId);
            } else {
                flagForManualReview(txnId);
            }
        }
    }

    private List<String> getOurPendingTransactions(String p) { return List.of(); }
    private String queryPartnerStatus(String p, String t) { return "COMPLETED"; }
    private void markComplete(String t) { }
    private void retryOrCompensate(String t) { }
    private void flagForManualReview(String t) { }
}
```

### What are common mistakes in this scenario?

The most common mistake is attempting two-phase commit across external REST APIs. The second is not using idempotency keys, which leads to double-debits on retry. The third is not having a bulkhead, so one down partner blocks all partners.

```java
package com.bank.payment.antipattern;

// WRONG: shared thread pool for all partners
public class SharedPoolWrong {
    // If HDFC is down, all 50 threads block on HDFC calls.
    // ICICI and SBI payments cannot proceed.
    // One partner's outage becomes a total outage.
}

// CORRECT: per-partner bulkhead (see PartnerBulkhead above)
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How do you handle the case where a partner processed the request but the response was lost? How do you handle a compensation that itself fails? What is the difference between an escrow hold and a direct debit? How does reconciliation handle timezone differences between partners? How do you prioritize reconciliation items?
